WEBVTT
TITLE Canonical Lr1 Items And Lalr Merging — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
SLR writes a reduce into every column of FOLLOW of the production head.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
So two states that finish the same production get the same reduce row.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
On S -> L = R | R, L -> * R | id, R -> L the state after L breaks it.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
One cell, a shift and a reduce: SLR conflicts, and LALR does not.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
An LR(1) item is a production with a dot, plus one terminal.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The lookahead says which terminal may follow the whole right-hand side.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Erase the lookahead and what is left is the core.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
State I3 of S -> C C holds 6 items over 3 cores; I0 holds 6 over 4.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Closure: for A -> alpha . B beta with lookahead a, add B productions.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
On S -> C C the kernel gives S -> . C C with lookahead $, so FIRST(C $) = {c, d}.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The same kernel produces six items, where the LR(0) closure produces three.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
In state I2 the second C is followed by the end marker, so there the lookahead is $.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
GOTO moves the dot over one symbol and closes again.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
On S -> C C the collection has 10 states; LR(0) has 7 on the same grammar.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
I3 and I6 hold the same items and differ only in lookahead.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The table built from these ten states accepts exactly the language of the grammar.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A completed item writes a reduce in its lookahead column, and nowhere else.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
So the LR(1) reduce row is always a subset of the SLR row.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Of the 27 completed items here, exactly one has a lookahead set strictly inside FOLLOW.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The extra terminal is the whole conflict: the state cannot be followed by it.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
LALR merges states with equal cores and unions their lookaheads.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
On S -> C C three merges take 10 states down to 7.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Here the union is exactly FOLLOW of C, so no cell gains a second action.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The LALR count is the LR(0) count, always: every core is one state.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
This grammar generates four strings: acd, bcd, ace and bce.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
After ac the state says B on e and A on d; after bc the two are swapped.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Merged, both lookaheads become d and e, so d and e each call two reductions.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Resolved in favour of the earlier production, the merged parser keeps 2 of the 4 strings.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A merge cannot create a shift/reduce conflict.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
A member that also holds the completed item was already in conflict.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Reduce/reduce is different: two members can hold different completed items.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
S -> A | B with A -> a and B -> a conflicts in LR(1) itself: 5 states, no merges.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Six grammars, four automata each: the page builds and counts all of them.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
SLR and LALR always tie; canonical LR(1) never has fewer states.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
GC is the witness for SLR below LALR: one shift/reduce for SLR, none for LALR.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
GB witnesses LALR below LR(1); GE and GF fail at LR(1) itself.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Five steps: grammar, FIRST, LR(0) automaton, canonical items, merge.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
If the canonical table already conflicts, stop: the grammar is not LR(1).

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
GATE 2005: S -> ( S ) | a gives 6 SLR, 10 canonical and 6 LALR states.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Four traps: counts tie, no new shift/reduce, LR(1) can conflict, counts are not power.

